(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','LIBRARY CHILD ROOM — Story time runs twice on Saturdays, at 10:00 a.m. and at 2:00 p.m. Children must be registered at the front desk before the first session, and an adult must stay in the room.','NONE','["A child must be registered and stay with an adult","A child must read a book aloud to the group","An adult must borrow a library card first","A place must be booked a month ahead"]','A',1,'The notice requires registration at the desk before the first session and an adult present in the room.',16),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','GROUP CHAT — Ines: "The volleyball trial has moved from the gym to Court 4 because of the dance rehearsal." — Tom: "Same time?" — Ines: "Yes, six o''clock, but bring outdoor shoes now."','NONE','["When the trial starts","Who must attend the trial","Where the trial takes place and what to wear","Whether players will be tested"]','C',1,'Only the location (Court 4 instead of the gym) and the footwear changed; the six o''clock start stays as it was.',17),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','RENTAL OFFICE — Your application is complete except for the employment form, which your employer must return to us directly rather than to you. We will assess the file as soon as that form arrives.','NONE','["A signed lease agreement","An employment form the employer must send to the office","A deposit payment","A photograph of the applicant"]','B',1,'The email names the employment form, which the employer must send to the office, as the only missing item.',18),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','MUSEUM LIFT — Out of service today. Please use the north stairwell, which is steep. The second-floor gallery stays open with reduced hours until 4:00 p.m.; all other floors keep their normal schedule.','NONE','["The museum will be closed for the day","Visitors should avoid the second floor","The lift will be repaired tomorrow morning","One gallery on the upper floor will close earlier than usual"]','D',1,'The lift is out and the stairwell replaces it; the second-floor gallery keeps reduced hours ending at 4:00 p.m., an earlier close.',19),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the instructions and answer the question.','CLUB FORM — Write in black ink only. Do not attach your own photograph; it is taken at the desk. Sign the bottom line only after the officer has checked your identification.','NONE','["Use a black pen and wait to sign until asked","Bring two printed photographs","Sign the form before filling in any field","Send the completed form by post"]','A',1,'Ink is limited to black, the photograph is taken at the desk, and signing waits until the officer checks identification.',20),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the flyer and answer the question.','CITY FARMERS MARKET — Open every Saturday from 7:00 a.m. until the stalls sell out, usually well before noon. Payment is by cash or market token, and tokens are sold at the machine by the gate.','NONE','["It accepts every kind of payment","It runs six days each week","The length of its opening on a given day is not fixed","It closes at exactly noon whatever is left"]','C',1,'Closing depends on when the stalls sell out, so the session ends at an unpredictable time; noon is only typical.',21),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the timetable note and answer the question.','TRAIN TIMETABLE, WINTER PERIOD — The 6:15 a.m. service to Harbourtown operates on school days only; on other weekdays the first train leaves at 6:45. Saturday services are unchanged.','NONE','["Every day of the week","On school days only","Every weekday without exception","On Saturdays only"]','B',1,'The 6:15 is limited to school days; other weekdays begin at 6:45 and Saturday times do not change.',22),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','TEXT MESSAGES — Dara: "My four o''clock shift was cancelled, so I can come to the film after all." — Nick: "Good, then we keep the 4:30 tickets."','NONE','["Dara wants to change the cinema time","Dara will buy the tickets","Nick has to work at 4:30","Dara is now free to join the film"]','D',1,'With the shift cancelled Dara can attend, so the 4:30 booking is kept rather than changed.',23),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the lab rule and answer the question.','BIOLOGY ROOM 3 — Wash your hands before leaving the laboratory, whether or not you handled specimens. Long hair must be tied back at every bench, and no drink is allowed near the microscopes.','NONE','["Hand washing before leaving the room","Handling a specimen each lesson","Drinking only at the back bench","Tying hair back only during experiments"]','A',1,'Hand washing applies to everyone leaving the lab regardless of specimen work, and hair must be tied back at every bench.',24),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','SPORTS MEDICINE — Your clearance letter is ready for collection. We cannot send it by email, because the school server rejects attachments from outside providers. Reception holds letters between 9:00 a.m. and 3:00 p.m.','NONE','["The letter still has to be written","Electronic delivery is blocked by the school server","The letter expires after today","Only the student may read the letter"]','B',1,'The clinic blames a server that rejects outside attachments, which is why collection at reception is necessary.',25),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the dormitory notice and answer the question.','HALL A CORRIDOR LIGHTS — From Thursday, corridor lighting dims to forty percent between midnight and 5:00 a.m. to reduce consumption. Room lighting and the lit study pods are unaffected; report a dark pod bulb to the office.','NONE','["When residents may use the study pods","How many bulbs each room has","The strength of the hallway lighting at night","The price of electricity in the hall"]','C',1,'The notice changes only the corridor brightness between midnight and 5:00 a.m., leaving rooms and pods unaffected.',26),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the instructions and answer the question.','ONLINE SURVEY — Twelve questions, about six minutes. Answers are stored without your name, and this link stops working on Sunday. Reloading the page will erase anything you have typed but not yet submitted.','NONE','["Nothing is lost","The survey closes for the whole week","Your name is added automatically","Answers typed but not submitted are cleared"]','D',1,'The instructions warn that a reload deletes unfinished entries; storage without a name is promised separately.',27),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the club flyer and answer the question.','SUSTAINABLE ARCHITECTURE CLUB — First meeting Thursday in hall 102. Both guest designers have confirmed the date. The talk is free, and no membership is needed simply to attend.','NONE','["Anyone may attend the talk at no cost","Attendance requires a paid membership","The date has not been settled","Visitors must be enrolled architects"]','A',1,'The flyer states the talk is free and that attendance needs no membership, with both speakers confirmed.',28),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the clinic statement and answer the question.','STATEMENT OF CHARGES — Item 1, consultation, is covered by your plan. Item 2, cast removal, is not on the plan list and is payable at the desk today. A receipt for your insurer can be printed on request.','NONE','["The consultation fee","Both listed items","The cast removal","Nothing, since a receipt will be issued"]','C',1,'Item 2, the cast removal, is excluded from the plan and must be paid at the desk; the consultation is covered.',29),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the message board and answer the question.','ARTS OFFICE — Rehearsal rooms are now booked through the online calendar rather than by email. Bookings open on the first day of each month, and a group may hold two rooms at a time.','NONE','["Emailing the office a week ahead","Using the online calendar when the month begins","Phoning on the morning required","Reserving only one room at a time"]','B',1,'Booking moved from email to the calendar, which opens on the first day of the month and allows two rooms.',30),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the pool notice and answer the question.','CITY POOL — The deep end requires a swim pass completed during this term. The test costs nothing and runs every Monday at 3:00 p.m. Sunday family session is held in the shallow end only.','NONE','["It costs five dollars","It is offered once a week at no charge","It takes place on Sunday evenings","It is needed for the shallow end"]','B',1,'The notice says the pass test is free and repeated each Monday at 3:00 p.m.; the deep end, not the shallow end, needs it.',31),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','STAFF CHAT — Hana: "The printer on floor five says out of order, and floor three is the only working one. It stands behind the glass door with the card reader." — Sam: "So I need my card to print?"','NONE','["To buy a new printer","To ask Hana to print the file","To use a card to enter the room holding the only working printer","To go to the machine on floor five"]','C',1,'Floor three has the only serviceable printer and it sits behind a card-reader door, which is why Sam needs the card.',32),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the schedule card and answer the question.','LECTURE SERIES — Every talk begins at 5:30 p.m. in the Amphitheatre. Doors open thirty minutes earlier, seating is unreserved, and anyone arriving late is shown to the back between items, not during one.','NONE','["Tickets must be collected in advance","Talks begin at 5:00 p.m.","Latecomers are refused entry","A seat is chosen when you arrive"]','D',1,'Because the seats are unreserved, attendees take whatever is free on arrival; doors open early and latecomers are seated between items.',33),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the instructions and answer the question.','BIKE REGISTRATION — Bring the frame number, stamped under the bottom bracket and difficult to read without a torch. Registration takes five minutes at the tent in the market on Saturday, and a sticker is then placed on the front fork.','NONE','["A number on the frame that is hard to see without a light","The original purchase receipt","A photograph of the owner","The height of the seat"]','A',1,'The instruction points to the frame number beneath the bottom bracket, which needs a torch to read.',34),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','GRADUATE SCHOOL — Your file lists two referees, but the requirement is three, and the invitation for the third was never sent because that field was left blank. Add a referee by Friday and an automatic deadline extension will follow.','NONE','["Submit a second essay","Name one more referee before Friday","Pay the application fee again","Request three separate extensions"]','B',1,'The missing third referee must be entered by Friday, after which the extension is applied automatically.',35),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage mainly discuss?','Mangroves are trees that grow where land meets the sea, in the warm muddy shallows of sheltered coasts. They survive saltwater that would kill most plants by filtering salt at their roots and by excreting what enters through their leaves. Their tangled prop roots trap sediment carried by rivers, so a stand of mangroves slowly builds new land outward, and during a storm the same roots break the energy of waves before it reaches the shore. Fishermen benefit directly, because many commercial species spend their young stages in the shade of the roots, safe from larger predators. Where mangroves have been cleared for shrimp ponds or seaside resorts, coastal erosion usually worsens within a few years and catches fall. Rebuilding them has proved harder than expected, because planted seedlings need the right mud, tidal range and fresh water, conditions that engineering rarely recreates.','NONE','["Why mangroves grow only in sheltered tropical mud","How mangroves build and shield coastlines and why replanting often fails","The life cycle of fish that live among mangrove roots","The economic value of shrimp ponds on cleared coasts"]','B',1,'The passage links root structure to land building and wave protection, then ends with why restoration is difficult.',36),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "trap" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Mangroves are trees that grow where land meets the sea, in the warm muddy shallows of sheltered coasts. They survive saltwater that would kill most plants by filtering salt at their roots and by excreting what enters through their leaves. Their tangled prop roots trap sediment carried by rivers, so a stand of mangroves slowly builds new land outward, and during a storm the same roots break the energy of waves before it reaches the shore. Fishermen benefit directly, because many commercial species spend their young stages in the shade of the roots, safe from larger predators. Where mangroves have been cleared for shrimp ponds or seaside resorts, coastal erosion usually worsens within a few years and catches fall. Rebuilding them has proved harder than expected, because planted seedlings need the right mud, tidal range and fresh water, conditions that engineering rarely recreates.','NONE','["catch and hold","carry away","break into pieces","hide from view"]','A',1,'The roots hold river sediment in place so new land accumulates, so "trap" = catch and hold.',37),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','It can be inferred from the passage that clearing mangroves for development tends to:','Mangroves are trees that grow where land meets the sea, in the warm muddy shallows of sheltered coasts. They survive saltwater that would kill most plants by filtering salt at their roots and by excreting what enters through their leaves. Their tangled prop roots trap sediment carried by rivers, so a stand of mangroves slowly builds new land outward, and during a storm the same roots break the energy of waves before it reaches the shore. Fishermen benefit directly, because many commercial species spend their young stages in the shade of the roots, safe from larger predators. Where mangroves have been cleared for shrimp ponds or seaside resorts, coastal erosion usually worsens within a few years and catches fall. Rebuilding them has proved harder than expected, because planted seedlings need the right mud, tidal range and fresh water, conditions that engineering rarely recreates.','NONE','["increase fish populations permanently","improve the mud quality for planted seedlings","reduce both shoreline stability and fishing yields","have no measurable effect on erosion"]','C',1,'Cleared sites show worse erosion and falling catches, so both protection and harvest decline.',38),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, what difficulty made paper notes attractive in Sichuan?','Long before any European government issued paper money, merchants in the Chinese region of Sichuan were already using it. Under the Song dynasty, which began in 960, currency there was heavy iron coin, so awkward to carry that a large purchase needed a cartload of metal. Records describe seventeen notes, each promising the bearer a set number of coins at a named shop, circulating instead of the coins themselves. The court first treated these notes as a convenient local habit, then recognised their value as a source of revenue and, in the early twelfth century, opened state workshops that printed official notes on mulberry bark paper with coloured ink and registered serial numbers. Forgery was punished severely, yet repeated overissue to pay for military spending undermined confidence, and the dynasty revised its note series so often that historians now trace monetary policy through the changing denominations.','NONE','["A shortage of gold and silver","A ban on private lending","The extreme weight of the iron coins in use","A fire that destroyed the mint records"]','C',1,'Iron coins were so heavy that a big purchase needed a cartload, which is why a written promise circulated.',39),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "undermined" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Long before any European government issued paper money, merchants in the Chinese region of Sichuan were already using it. Under the Song dynasty, which began in 960, currency there was heavy iron coin, so awkward to carry that a large purchase needed a cartload of metal. Records describe seventeen notes, each promising the bearer a set number of coins at a named shop, circulating instead of the coins themselves. The court first treated these notes as a convenient local habit, then recognised their value as a source of revenue and, in the early twelfth century, opened state workshops that printed official notes on mulberry bark paper with coloured ink and registered serial numbers. Forgery was punished severely, yet repeated overissue to pay for military spending undermined confidence, and the dynasty revised its note series so often that historians now trace monetary policy through the changing denominations.','NONE','["strengthened","weakened","recorded","paid for"]','B',1,'Printing extra notes to fund war eroded trust in the currency, so "undermined" = weakened.',40),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage suggest about the Song state''s later involvement?','Long before any European government issued paper money, merchants in the Chinese region of Sichuan were already using it. Under the Song dynasty, which began in 960, currency there was heavy iron coin, so awkward to carry that a large purchase needed a cartload of metal. Records describe seventeen notes, each promising the bearer a set number of coins at a named shop, circulating instead of the coins themselves. The court first treated these notes as a convenient local habit, then recognised their value as a source of revenue and, in the early twelfth century, opened state workshops that printed official notes on mulberry bark paper with coloured ink and registered serial numbers. Forgery was punished severely, yet repeated overissue to pay for military spending undermined confidence, and the dynasty revised its note series so often that historians now trace monetary policy through the changing denominations.','NONE','["It took over note production partly because the notes raised money for the state","It forbade merchants from using any written promise","It stopped using coins after 960","It exported its notes to Europe"]','A',1,'The court saw the notes as a revenue source and then opened its own printing workshops, implying fiscal motive.',41),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, why do some city birds sing at a higher pitch than woodland birds?','Urban birds sing differently from the same species in quiet woodland, and traffic noise is the usual explanation. Low frequencies travel far between buildings, and engine hum fills exactly the low band that many songs use, so a bird singing low is drowned out. Researchers have found that some city populations raise the lowest frequency of their song, while others simply sing at dawn, when traffic is lighter and their notes carry further. Singing louder also costs energy, and experiments that replay traffic noise to wild birds show shorter phrases and fewer repeated motifs, changes that matter because females often judge a male by how steadily he delivers a song. The effect is not only acoustic: where night traffic is constant, birds may abandon nesting sites that offer abundant food, so a quiet habitat has become a resource that has to be competed for.','NONE','["Low notes are lost in the deep hum of traffic","High notes use less energy than low ones","Females prefer the lowest available pitch","Sound travels faster through built-up ground"]','A',1,'Engine noise occupies the low band of a song, so a low-pitched bird is drowned out and a higher one is heard.',42),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "carry" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Urban birds sing differently from the same species in quiet woodland, and traffic noise is the usual explanation. Low frequencies travel far between buildings, and engine hum fills exactly the low band that many songs use, so a bird singing low is drowned out. Researchers have found that some city populations raise the lowest frequency of their song, while others simply sing at dawn, when traffic is lighter and their notes carry further. Singing louder also costs energy, and experiments that replay traffic noise to wild birds show shorter phrases and fewer repeated motifs, changes that matter because females often judge a male by how steadily he delivers a song. The effect is not only acoustic: where night traffic is constant, birds may abandon nesting sites that offer abundant food, so a quiet habitat has become a resource that has to be competed for.','NONE','["lift","repeat","travel","attract"]','C',1,'Singing at dawn lets a bird''s notes reach further through quieter air, so "carry" = travel.',43),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about males whose songs are changed by noise?','Urban birds sing differently from the same species in quiet woodland, and traffic noise is the usual explanation. Low frequencies travel far between buildings, and engine hum fills exactly the low band that many songs use, so a bird singing low is drowned out. Researchers have found that some city populations raise the lowest frequency of their song, while others simply sing at dawn, when traffic is lighter and their notes carry further. Singing louder also costs energy, and experiments that replay traffic noise to wild birds show shorter phrases and fewer repeated motifs, changes that matter because females often judge a male by how steadily he delivers a song. The effect is not only acoustic: where night traffic is constant, birds may abandon nesting sites that offer abundant food, so a quiet habitat has become a resource that has to be competed for.','NONE','["Females will prefer them because they sing louder","Their broken phrases may tell females less about the singer","Noise changes song but never affects breeding","Males lengthen their phrases when traffic rises"]','B',1,'Females judge steadiness of delivery, so noisier, shorter and less repeated song conveys less information about the male.',44),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage say about the earliest oat crops?','Oats were the last major cereal to be domesticated, and they arrived in the field uninvited. When farmers in the Fertile Crescent grew wheat and barley, wild oat seeds fell among the grain and were carried home, threshed, and sown again with the crop. The plant was therefore a weed in wheat before it was a crop in its own right. Its adoption made sense in cooler, wetter northern Europe, where wheat yields poorly while oats put up with damp soil and a short season. Because oats keep their grain inside a tough husk, they store well through a long winter, an advantage for communities without dry granaries. The archaeological record shows the change in reverse: once oats were planted deliberately, storage improved as well. The larger shift may have come after harvesting, since hulled oats soften only after long heating and stirring, a method that suits households with fuel to spare.','NONE','["They were planted deliberately in river valleys","They reached Europe from the Americas","They were first sown by accident among wheat and barley","They were rejected because they stored badly"]','C',1,'Wild oat seeds fell into harvested grain and were resown with it, making oats a weed before a crop.',45),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "put up with" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Oats were the last major cereal to be domesticated, and they arrived in the field uninvited. When farmers in the Fertile Crescent grew wheat and barley, wild oat seeds fell among the grain and were carried home, threshed, and sown again with the crop. The plant was therefore a weed in wheat before it was a crop in its own right. Its adoption made sense in cooler, wetter northern Europe, where wheat yields poorly while oats put up with damp soil and a short season. Because oats keep their grain inside a tough husk, they store well through a long winter, an advantage for communities without dry granaries. The archaeological record shows the change in reverse: once oats were planted deliberately, storage improved as well. The larger shift may have come after harvesting, since hulled oats soften only after long heating and stirring, a method that suits households with fuel to spare.','NONE','["endure","benefit from","grow quickly in","refuse to grow in"]','A',1,'Oats succeed in wet, cool ground that wheat dislikes, so "put up with" = endure.',46),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage imply about preparing oats as food?','Oats were the last major cereal to be domesticated, and they arrived in the field uninvited. When farmers in the Fertile Crescent grew wheat and barley, wild oat seeds fell among the grain and were carried home, threshed, and sown again with the crop. The plant was therefore a weed in wheat before it was a crop in its own right. Its adoption made sense in cooler, wetter northern Europe, where wheat yields poorly while oats put up with damp soil and a short season. Because oats keep their grain inside a tough husk, they store well through a long winter, an advantage for communities without dry granaries. The archaeological record shows the change in reverse: once oats were planted deliberately, storage improved as well. The larger shift may have come after harvesting, since hulled oats soften only after long heating and stirring, a method that suits households with fuel to spare.','NONE','["They could be eaten raw once the husk was removed","Their preparation assumed time and fuel to burn","They were simpler to cook than wheat","They needed no storage facilities at all"]','B',1,'Long heating and stirring are required, so oats became useful only where households could afford fuel and labour.',47),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What is the main point of the passage?','A dictionary and a glossary both match words to meanings, yet they answer different questions. A dictionary is built for a whole language: it records every word a reader may meet, gives several senses for each entry, adds pronunciation and origin, and illustrates use with quotations. A glossary is built for a limited text, course, or profession, and it lists only the words present there, usually with one short definition chosen for that context. The difference is not simply size but direction. A dictionary describes how a language is used; a glossary tries to make a particular document understandable. That explains why a legal glossary defines consideration without mentioning thought or payment, senses a dictionary must include, and why a glossary compiled for one novel is of no use for another. For a learner, a glossary lowers the barrier to a single text, while a dictionary builds knowledge that lasts beyond it.','NONE','["The two tools serve different needs and are organised around different material","A dictionary is more useful than a glossary for any reader","Glossaries have replaced dictionaries in the classroom","Both tools list every sense of a word"]','A',1,'The passage contrasts purpose and scope, noting the difference is direction rather than mere length.',48),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "compiled" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','A dictionary and a glossary both match words to meanings, yet they answer different questions. A dictionary is built for a whole language: it records every word a reader may meet, gives several senses for each entry, adds pronunciation and origin, and illustrates use with quotations. A glossary is built for a limited text, course, or profession, and it lists only the words present there, usually with one short definition chosen for that context. The difference is not simply size but direction. A dictionary describes how a language is used; a glossary tries to make a particular document understandable. That explains why a legal glossary defines consideration without mentioning thought or payment, senses a dictionary must include, and why a glossary compiled for one novel is of no use for another. For a learner, a glossary lowers the barrier to a single text, while a dictionary builds knowledge that lasts beyond it.','NONE','["printed","translated","put together","published"]','C',1,'A glossary gathered for a specific novel, so "compiled" = put together.',49),
(@set03_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','Why does the passage mention the legal term "consideration"?','A dictionary and a glossary both match words to meanings, yet they answer different questions. A dictionary is built for a whole language: it records every word a reader may meet, gives several senses for each entry, adds pronunciation and origin, and illustrates use with quotations. A glossary is built for a limited text, course, or profession, and it lists only the words present there, usually with one short definition chosen for that context. The difference is not simply size but direction. A dictionary describes how a language is used; a glossary tries to make a particular document understandable. That explains why a legal glossary defines consideration without mentioning thought or payment, senses a dictionary must include, and why a glossary compiled for one novel is of no use for another. For a learner, a glossary lowers the barrier to a single text, while a dictionary builds knowledge that lasts beyond it.','NONE','["To argue that legal writing is hard for students","To show that a glossary keeps only the sense its own text needs","To prove that dictionaries omit technical words","To compare two systems of law"]','B',1,'The term illustrates how a glossary selects the single contextual sense, leaving other dictionary senses aside.',50);
INSERT INTO tp_questions (exam_id, section, task_type, prompt, body_text, audio_mode, choices_json, answer_key, auto_score, explanation, order_no) VALUES
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Could you hold the elevator while I lock the laboratory door?','TTS','["It is on the third floor.","Yes, at three o''clock.","I already locked it.","Sure, I will wait right here."]','D',1,'A request for help is best answered by agreeing to wait, which option D does.',51),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','When does the registrar accept the last course change?','TTS','["About twice a year.","Yes, I changed it.","At the end of the day on Friday.","You should bring your student card."]','C',1,'A question about a deadline needs a time, and only C gives Friday at close of business.',52),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','I did not expect the statistics exam to be that long.','TTS','["So do I.","Neither did I, honestly.","It is held on Thursday.","You are expected to study more."]','B',1,'A past-tense expectation is agreed with "neither did I", matching B.',53),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Would you rather present first or last in the seminar?','TTS','["First, so I can finish early.","It was quite good.","About twenty minutes.","Yes, I would."]','A',1,'An either/or preference needs a choice, which A supplies with a reason.',54),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Has anyone repaired the projector in lecture hall two?','TTS','["Yes, it is a new model.","The lecture begins at nine.","I have two projectors.","An engineer fixed it this morning."]','D',1,'A yes/no question about repair is answered by stating who repaired it and when.',55),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Why did the field trip leave before sunrise?','TTS','["By bus, of course.","We have been there before.","To reach the quarry before the heat.","Yes, it left before eight."]','C',1,'A why question needs a reason, and C explains the early departure.',56),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Do you mind if I return this book next week instead?','TTS','["The book is in my bag.","Not at all, that is fine.","It has ten chapters.","Yes, I did."]','B',1,'A request about a preference is answered with permission, as B grants it.',57),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','How was the interview with the archive director?','TTS','["Far more interesting than I expected.","In the basement of the old library.","Two interviews are planned.","It lasted an hour."]','A',1,'A how question about an experience asks for an evaluation, which A gives.',58),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Where did you put the box of handouts?','TTS','["They were printed this morning.","I did not need them.","Those handouts were useful.","Under the desk beside the printer."]','D',1,'A where question requires a location, supplied only by D.',59),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','The registration website keeps rejecting my password.','TTS','["I have registered as well.","Yes, it is a long password.","Try the reset link under the sign-in box.","Registration costs nothing."]','C',1,'A complaint about a failed login is met best with a suggested remedy.',60),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Shall we skip the café and study in the quiet room?','TTS','["I like the café very much.","That works better for me.","The room seats twelve people.","Yes, we did."]','B',1,'A joint proposal is accepted or redirected, which B does by agreeing with the alternative.',61),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Whose notes are written on the back of this plan?','TTS','["I think they belong to Nadia.","The plan is on the table.","Notes were taken in May.","It is a good plan."]','A',1,'A whose question asks about a person, and only A names an owner.',62),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Did you finish the reading for the discussion group?','TTS','["The reading was assigned twice.","I read part of the plan.","The group meets on Wednesday.","Almost, though the last article was difficult."]','D',1,'A yes/no question about completion is answered with a degree of completion, as D does.',63),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','How often does the science café hold its evening talks?','TTS','["I enjoyed one of the talks.","It is a monthly ticket.","Roughly every third week.","Each talk lasts forty minutes."]','C',1,'How often asks for a frequency, which C provides; D answers length instead.',64),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','I am not sure my abstract is short enough.','TTS','["The limit is on the website.","Mine was even longer than yours.","I sent mine yesterday.","Nobody checked the length."]','B',1,'A worry about length is met with reassurance comparing the same thing, which B offers.',65),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Would you like a seat at the geography fair tomorrow?','TTS','["Yes, if there is still one free.","The fair is in the east hall.","It begins at noon.","I studied geography once."]','A',1,'An offer of a seat is answered by accepting it conditionally, as A does.',66),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','What happened to the coffee machine on floor two?','TTS','["Someone cleaned it yesterday.","It makes very good coffee.","I bought a machine last year.","A technician unplugged it after a leak."]','D',1,'A what happened question asks for an event, and D reports the technician''s action.',67),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Can you send me the figures from your survey?','TTS','["I will do it this afternoon.","There are six figures in all.","The survey closed in June.","It was quite a good survey."]','A',1,'A can you request for a task is answered by agreeing to perform it, as A does.',68),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I have six books on hold for my thesis chapter, and two of them are due tomorrow. Can I keep them longer? — Librarian: Ordinary loans renew online twice, but those two are on course reserve, so they leave the building for only two hours at a time. — Student: Can I at least reserve them again right after I return them? — Librarian: Yes, and I will put your name first on the shelf list so you get the next copy.','TTS','["Two of the items she borrowed belong to a short-term reserve list.","The library has mislaid her thesis chapter.","She has unpaid fines on two books.","The librarian has closed her account."]','A',1,'The librarian explains that the two due tomorrow are course reserve items with a two-hour borrowing window.',69),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I have six books on hold for my thesis chapter, and two of them are due tomorrow. Can I keep them longer? — Librarian: Ordinary loans renew online twice, but those two are on course reserve, so they leave the building for only two hours at a time. — Student: Can I at least reserve them again right after I return them? — Librarian: Yes, and I will put your name first on the shelf list so you get the next copy.','TTS','["Extend the reserve loans for two weeks","Charge a fee for the extra reservations","Give her priority for the next available copy","Move the books to a different floor"]','C',1,'The librarian agrees to place her name first on the shelf list, i.e. to give her priority for the next copy.',70),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I have six books on hold for my thesis chapter, and two of them are due tomorrow. Can I keep them longer? — Librarian: Ordinary loans renew online twice, but those two are on course reserve, so they leave the building for only two hours at a time. — Student: Can I at least reserve them again right after I return them? — Librarian: Yes, and I will put your name first on the shelf list so you get the next copy.','TTS','["She will abandon the thesis topic","She intends to keep working on the chapter in the coming days","She will buy her own copies of the reserve books","She plans to return all six books and stop"]','B',1,'Arranging to get the next copy shows she expects to continue the same work soon.',71),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: Professor Alvarez, the wetland permit has not arrived, and our sampling day is nine days away. — Professor: The county office will not even open the request until it has the site map, and you sent them only a written description. — Student: I thought the map came from this office. — Professor: It came from me last term, so I will forward it today, and you should attach the safety form at the same time.','TTS','["The office is closed for nine days.","The professor refused to sign the request.","The sampling date has already passed.","A required map was never attached to the request"]','D',1,'The professor says the county office will not open the request without the site map, which was not attached.',72),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: Professor Alvarez, the wetland permit has not arrived, and our sampling day is nine days away. — Professor: The county office will not even open the request until it has the site map, and you sent them only a written description. — Student: I thought the map came from this office. — Professor: It came from me last term, so I will forward it today, and you should attach the safety form at the same time.','TTS','["Attach the safety form to the request","Book a new sampling day","Ask the county office for a fee waiver","Cancel the fieldwork"]','A',1,'The student is told to attach the safety form when the forwarded map is sent.',73),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: Professor Alvarez, the wetland permit has not arrived, and our sampling day is nine days away. — Professor: The county office will not even open the request until it has the site map, and you sent them only a written description. — Student: I thought the map came from this office. — Professor: It came from me last term, so I will forward it today, and you should attach the safety form at the same time.','TTS','["She will go to the county office in person","She no longer supports the sampling plan","She had already supplied a document the student thought was missing","She wants the student to delay the project"]','C',1,'The professor sent the map last term, so the document the student expected to be missing already existed.',74),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Woman: Our presentation is on Tuesday, and I recorded yesterday''s practice, but the file will not open on my laptop. — Man: Mine opened after I moved it into the phone app. Do we both still need to speak? — Woman: The rubric says each section must have a different speaker, so I will take methods and you take results. — Man: Fine, but send me your slides tonight so I can match the wording.','TTS','["She missed the practice session entirely.","A practice recording would not play on her computer.","Her slides are missing from the file.","The presentation has moved to Wednesday."]','B',1,'The woman states that the file from yesterday''s recorded practice will not open on her laptop.',75),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Woman: Our presentation is on Tuesday, and I recorded yesterday''s practice, but the file will not open on my laptop. — Man: Mine opened after I moved it into the phone app. Do we both still need to speak? — Woman: The rubric says each section must have a different speaker, so I will take methods and you take results. — Man: Fine, but send me your slides tonight so I can match the wording.','TTS','["It should be deleted and re-recorded","It must be emailed to the professor","The recording is not needed","Copying it into the phone app opened it for him"]','D',1,'The man reports that his own file opened once he moved it into the phone application.',76),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Woman: Our presentation is on Tuesday, and I recorded yesterday''s practice, but the file will not open on my laptop. — Man: Mine opened after I moved it into the phone app. Do we both still need to speak? — Woman: The rubric says each section must have a different speaker, so I will take methods and you take results. — Man: Fine, but send me your slides tonight so I can match the wording.','TTS','["Different speakers for different sections","A single speaker for the whole talk","A written script handed in early","Slides shared a week in advance"]','A',1,'The woman cites the rule that every section needs a different speaker, splitting methods and results.',77),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: The health clinic gave me a transport voucher, and I assumed I could use it here for a wheel repair. — Clerk: It covers the bus and the shared bikes, and repairs are billed separately, so the voucher will not apply. — Student: Is there any student discount on the work? — Clerk: Twenty percent on labour, but parts are charged at cost, and I can order the tyre by Thursday if you sign the request now.','TTS','["It must be renewed each term","It covers parts but not labour","It pays for transit use but not for repair work","It can be exchanged for the discount"]','C',1,'The clerk says the voucher is for the bus and shared bikes, so it does not pay for repairs.',78),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: The health clinic gave me a transport voucher, and I assumed I could use it here for a wheel repair. — Clerk: It covers the bus and the shared bikes, and repairs are billed separately, so the voucher will not apply. — Student: Is there any student discount on the work? — Clerk: Twenty percent on labour, but parts are charged at cost, and I can order the tyre by Thursday if you sign the request now.','TTS','["It applies to both labour and parts","It lowers the service charge but not the materials cost","It is worth more than the voucher","It requires a new membership"]','B',1,'The discount takes twenty percent off labour, while parts remain at cost.',79),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: The health clinic gave me a transport voucher, and I assumed I could use it here for a wheel repair. — Clerk: It covers the bus and the shared bikes, and repairs are billed separately, so the voucher will not apply. — Student: Is there any student discount on the work? — Clerk: Twenty percent on labour, but parts are charged at cost, and I can order the tyre by Thursday if you sign the request now.','TTS','["Pay the whole repair bill today","Bring the bicycle to the clinic instead","Wait until the following week","Sign the order request so the part can be ordered"]','D',1,'The clerk will order the tyre by Thursday only if the student signs the request now.',80),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Good afternoon. The campus bus fleet will use the temporary stop beside the sports field from Monday until the crosswalk at the main gate is repaved, work expected to last three weeks. Regular timetables are unchanged, and the extra evening shuttle will still run at ten fifteen. Riders who need a vehicle with a ramp should telephone dispatch in the morning, because only two such buses join the route.','TTS','["A new bus fare","A temporary change of where the buses stop","A cancelled evening shuttle","A repaved crosswalk at the gate"]','B',1,'The announcement concerns buses using a temporary stop beside the sports field until the work finishes.',81),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Good afternoon. The campus bus fleet will use the temporary stop beside the sports field from Monday until the crosswalk at the main gate is repaved, work expected to last three weeks. Regular timetables are unchanged, and the extra evening shuttle will still run at ten fifteen. Riders who need a vehicle with a ramp should telephone dispatch in the morning, because only two such buses join the route.','TTS','["Until Monday morning","Ten fifteen every night","The whole term","About three weeks"]','D',1,'The repaving work, and with it the temporary stop, is expected to last three weeks.',82),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Good afternoon. The campus bus fleet will use the temporary stop beside the sports field from Monday until the crosswalk at the main gate is repaved, work expected to last three weeks. Regular timetables are unchanged, and the extra evening shuttle will still run at ten fifteen. Riders who need a vehicle with a ramp should telephone dispatch in the morning, because only two such buses join the route.','TTS','["Telephone dispatch during the morning","Arrive early at the main gate","Ride the evening shuttle instead","Pay an additional charge"]','A',1,'Because only two ramp-equipped buses run, those riders are told to call dispatch in the morning.',83),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention please. The dining hall will close early at two o''clock on Friday for a scheduled equipment inspection, and the ground-floor café will stay open until six. Students on a full meal plan may collect a boxed lunch from the west door before noon on the same day. Saturday breakfast will be served in the hall from seven as usual.','TTS','["The café will be closed for the day","The meal plan is suspended on Friday","The main dining hall closes earlier than usual","Saturday breakfast will be cancelled"]','C',1,'Friday''s dining hall shuts at two for the inspection, while the café continues to six.',84),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention please. The dining hall will close early at two o''clock on Friday for a scheduled equipment inspection, and the ground-floor café will stay open until six. Students on a full meal plan may collect a boxed lunch from the west door before noon on the same day. Saturday breakfast will be served in the hall from seven as usual.','TTS','["Any visitor to the café","Students holding a full meal plan","Only residents of the dormitories","Students who order a week ahead"]','B',1,'The boxed lunch is offered to students on a full meal plan, collected before noon.',85),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','Attention please. The dining hall will close early at two o''clock on Friday for a scheduled equipment inspection, and the ground-floor café will stay open until six. Students on a full meal plan may collect a boxed lunch from the west door before noon on the same day. Saturday breakfast will be served in the hall from seven as usual.','TTS','["It will continue through the weekend","It replaces the Saturday breakfast service","It affects the café as well","It was arranged in advance rather than forced by an emergency"]','D',1,'The announcement calls it a scheduled inspection and normal service resumes Saturday, implying a planned event.',86),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','This is the housing office. Residents of Cedar Hall must remove bicycles from the ground-floor corridor by Friday evening. Bikes left after that will be tagged and moved to the yard behind the laundry building, where they will remain for one month before disposal. The corridor has to stay clear because the fire board inspects the building next Monday, and rack space in the basement needs no permit.','TTS','["By Friday evening","By Monday morning","Within one month","Before the end of the term"]','A',1,'The deadline stated for clearing the corridor is Friday evening.',87),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','This is the housing office. Residents of Cedar Hall must remove bicycles from the ground-floor corridor by Friday evening. Bikes left after that will be tagged and moved to the yard behind the laundry building, where they will remain for one month before disposal. The corridor has to stay clear because the fire board inspects the building next Monday, and rack space in the basement needs no permit.','TTS','["The racks are being repainted","The laundry building is closing","A safety inspection of the building is due","Bicycles are now banned from the hall"]','C',1,'The stated reason for a clear corridor is the fire board''s inspection on Monday.',88),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question.','This is the housing office. Residents of Cedar Hall must remove bicycles from the ground-floor corridor by Friday evening. Bikes left after that will be tagged and moved to the yard behind the laundry building, where they will remain for one month before disposal. The corridor has to stay clear because the fire board inspects the building next Monday, and rack space in the basement needs no permit.','TTS','["It will be sold immediately","It is moved to a yard and kept there for a month","The owner is fined on the spot","It is locked to a basement rack"]','B',1,'Late bikes are tagged, moved to the yard behind the laundry building, and held a month before disposal.',89),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Today let us look at why so many crops depend on a handful of varieties, and what is being done about it. For most of agricultural history, farmers saved seed locally, and each village carried its own strains of wheat or rice, adapted to local soil and rainfall. With industrial seed supply, those local strains were abandoned, and in many countries two or three high-yielding varieties now cover most of the acreage. Genetic uniformity is convenient for buyers but dangerous for growers, because a single disease can find every plant equally edible, as the nineteenth-century potato failure showed. Seed banks answer this by drying and freezing samples of thousands of strains at low temperature, where they can lie dormant for decades. A bank is not a museum, however: the stored strains must be grown out periodically, because drying damages some seeds, and because the traits that made a strain useful may include how it responds to a season a freezer cannot store.','TTS','["How farmers selected wheat in the past","Why industrial seed is cheaper for buyers","The danger of uniform crops and how stored seed diversity helps","The history of the nineteenth-century potato failure"]','C',1,'The talk moves from the risk of uniform acreage to seed banks as a way of keeping many strains available.',90),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Today let us look at why so many crops depend on a handful of varieties, and what is being done about it. For most of agricultural history, farmers saved seed locally, and each village carried its own strains of wheat or rice, adapted to local soil and rainfall. With industrial seed supply, those local strains were abandoned, and in many countries two or three high-yielding varieties now cover most of the acreage. Genetic uniformity is convenient for buyers but dangerous for growers, because a single disease can find every plant equally edible, as the nineteenth-century potato failure showed. Seed banks answer this by drying and freezing samples of thousands of strains at low temperature, where they can lie dormant for decades. A bank is not a museum, however: the stored strains must be grown out periodically, because drying damages some seeds, and because the traits that made a strain useful may include how it responds to a season a freezer cannot store.','TTS','["A single disease can attack every plant because none resist it","Uniform crops need far more water","Buyers refuse to purchase uniform varieties","Uniform strains cannot survive freezing"]','A',1,'The speaker explains that uniformity is dangerous because one disease finds every plant equally edible.',91),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Today let us look at why so many crops depend on a handful of varieties, and what is being done about it. For most of agricultural history, farmers saved seed locally, and each village carried its own strains of wheat or rice, adapted to local soil and rainfall. With industrial seed supply, those local strains were abandoned, and in many countries two or three high-yielding varieties now cover most of the acreage. Genetic uniformity is convenient for buyers but dangerous for growers, because a single disease can find every plant equally edible, as the nineteenth-century potato failure showed. Seed banks answer this by drying and freezing samples of thousands of strains at low temperature, where they can lie dormant for decades. A bank is not a museum, however: the stored strains must be grown out periodically, because drying damages some seeds, and because the traits that made a strain useful may include how it responds to a season a freezer cannot store.','TTS','["Its shelves hold far too many strains","Its funding comes only from farmers","It displays seeds for visitors rather than using them","Its strains have to be planted and renewed, not simply stored"]','D',1,'The speaker insists a bank is not a museum because stored strains must be grown out from time to time.',92),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Today let us look at why so many crops depend on a handful of varieties, and what is being done about it. For most of agricultural history, farmers saved seed locally, and each village carried its own strains of wheat or rice, adapted to local soil and rainfall. With industrial seed supply, those local strains were abandoned, and in many countries two or three high-yielding varieties now cover most of the acreage. Genetic uniformity is convenient for buyers but dangerous for growers, because a single disease can find every plant equally edible, as the nineteenth-century potato failure showed. Seed banks answer this by drying and freezing samples of thousands of strains at low temperature, where they can lie dormant for decades. A bank is not a museum, however: the stored strains must be grown out periodically, because drying damages some seeds, and because the traits that made a strain useful may include how it responds to a season a freezer cannot store.','TTS','["They can be copied by hand in the laboratory","They become visible only when a strain is actually grown","They matter little to plant breeders","They damage the seeds held in storage"]','B',1,'Since a freezer cannot store a season, response to seasons shows only when the strain is grown out.',93),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Let us consider a material that has been underwater for two thousand years and is still holding. Roman builders poured harbour concrete from lime, volcanic ash, and seawater, and marine samples now show a mineral called aluminium tobermorite growing inside them. That matters because tobermorite is hard and interlocking, so the rock becomes stronger as it ages rather than weaker. Modern engineers treated the effect as a curiosity until a recent study followed synthetic mixtures in the laboratory: seawater moving through the cracks keeps reacting with the ash, and the crystals fill the gaps that would otherwise widen. Ordinary concrete, by contrast, cracks when its reinforcing steel rusts, and the cracks then admit more water. The Romans had no steel to rust, and their mixture was soft enough for seawater to pass through. Researchers now hope the lesson may cut the cement industry''s own emissions, since a concrete that lasts twice as long needs half as many replacements.','TTS','["A hard mineral that continues to grow inside the material","Large pieces of rusted reinforcing steel","Volcanic ash that never reacted with the water","A modern synthetic additive"]','A',1,'The samples show aluminium tobermorite forming within the old concrete over time.',94),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Let us consider a material that has been underwater for two thousand years and is still holding. Roman builders poured harbour concrete from lime, volcanic ash, and seawater, and marine samples now show a mineral called aluminium tobermorite growing inside them. That matters because tobermorite is hard and interlocking, so the rock becomes stronger as it ages rather than weaker. Modern engineers treated the effect as a curiosity until a recent study followed synthetic mixtures in the laboratory: seawater moving through the cracks keeps reacting with the ash, and the crystals fill the gaps that would otherwise widen. Ordinary concrete, by contrast, cracks when its reinforcing steel rusts, and the cracks then admit more water. The Romans had no steel to rust, and their mixture was soft enough for seawater to pass through. Researchers now hope the lesson may cut the cement industry''s own emissions, since a concrete that lasts twice as long needs half as many replacements.','TTS','["It sets far faster underwater","It cannot be poured in a harbour","Its lime content is much lower","Rusting metal inside it widens cracks that let more water in"]','D',1,'Ordinary concrete cracks when its steel rusts, and the resulting gaps admit further water.',95),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Let us consider a material that has been underwater for two thousand years and is still holding. Roman builders poured harbour concrete from lime, volcanic ash, and seawater, and marine samples now show a mineral called aluminium tobermorite growing inside them. That matters because tobermorite is hard and interlocking, so the rock becomes stronger as it ages rather than weaker. Modern engineers treated the effect as a curiosity until a recent study followed synthetic mixtures in the laboratory: seawater moving through the cracks keeps reacting with the ash, and the crystals fill the gaps that would otherwise widen. Ordinary concrete, by contrast, cracks when its reinforcing steel rusts, and the cracks then admit more water. The Romans had no steel to rust, and their mixture was soft enough for seawater to pass through. Researchers now hope the lesson may cut the cement industry''s own emissions, since a concrete that lasts twice as long needs half as many replacements.','TTS','["confess","allow in","measure","block"]','B',1,'The cracks take more water in, so "admit" here means allow in.',96),
(@set03_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question.','Let us consider a material that has been underwater for two thousand years and is still holding. Roman builders poured harbour concrete from lime, volcanic ash, and seawater, and marine samples now show a mineral called aluminium tobermorite growing inside them. That matters because tobermorite is hard and interlocking, so the rock becomes stronger as it ages rather than weaker. Modern engineers treated the effect as a curiosity until a recent study followed synthetic mixtures in the laboratory: seawater moving through the cracks keeps reacting with the ash, and the crystals fill the gaps that would otherwise widen. Ordinary concrete, by contrast, cracks when its reinforcing steel rusts, and the cracks then admit more water. The Romans had no steel to rust, and their mixture was soft enough for seawater to pass through. Researchers now hope the lesson may cut the cement industry''s own emissions, since a concrete that lasts twice as long needs half as many replacements.','TTS','["Harbour walls should be rebuilt with steel bars","The laboratory work will have to be abandoned","Studying the old mixtures may help reduce replacements and emissions","Ancient builders understood their chemistry formally"]','C',1,'The closing hope about emissions and fewer replacements implies practical value for today''s industry.',97);
INSERT INTO tp_questions (exam_id, section, task_type, prompt, body_text, audio_mode, choices_json, answer_key, auto_score, explanation, order_no) VALUES
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["to","asked","submit","Students","draft","are","one","by","Friday"]','students are asked to submit one draft by friday',1,'Passive subject (Students are asked) + infinitive (to submit one draft) + time phrase (by Friday).',98),
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["review","committee","next","The","proposal","every","month","will"]','the committee will review every proposal next month',1,'Subject (The committee) + future verb (will review) + object (every proposal) + time phrase (next month).',99),
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["as","surfaces","panels","efficiency","their","lose","Solar","collect","dust"]','solar panels lose efficiency as their surfaces collect dust',1,'Main clause (Solar panels lose efficiency) + causal clause introduced by as (their surfaces collect dust).',100),
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["studying","She","noise","of","been","has","the","effect","since","March"]','she has been studying the effect of noise since march',1,'Present perfect continuous (has been studying) + object (the effect of noise) + since phrase (since March).',101),
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["received","questions","Each","printed","copy","of","the","participant","the"]','each participant received the printed copy of the questions',1,'Subject (Each participant) + past verb (received) + noun phrase with of-phrase (the printed copy of the questions).',102),
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["stay","The","open","library","period","will","examination","throughout","the"]','the library will stay open throughout the examination period',1,'Subject (The library) + will stay open + length phrase (throughout the examination period).',103),
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["temperatures","record","We","before","should","the","bench","sun","heats","the"]','we should record temperatures before the sun heats the bench',1,'Subject (We) + modal (should record temperatures) + time clause (before the sun heats the bench).',104),
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["explain","changed","sample","the","could","second","why","Nobody","had"]','nobody could explain why the second sample had changed',1,'Subject (Nobody) + modal (could explain) + embedded clause with normal order (why the second sample had changed).',105),
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_EMAIL','Write an email to your dormitory supervisor (about 50-80 words). You have locked your keys inside your room and your floor card reader is not working either. Explain what happened, say when you will be back, and ask whether staff can let you in later tonight.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review; look for a clear greeting, the two problems, a time, and a polite request.',106),
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_EMAIL','Write an email to your course instructor (about 50-80 words). Your group discovered that the data set posted for the statistics project contains duplicated rows. Describe the problem briefly and ask whether the file will be replaced before the deadline.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review; a specific description of the duplicated rows plus one clear question scores well.',107),
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_DISCUSSION','Your university plans to close the on-campus cafeteria and enlarge the library reading area instead. Read the two viewpoints and write a response (about 100-115 words) in which you state your own opinion and give a reason or example. Devin: "Students can order food in twenty minutes from the street outside, so the space is wasted anyway." — Priya: "A cafeteria is where students from different programmes actually meet; no reading room does that."',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review; address one of the two viewpoints and add your own reason or example.',108),
(@set03_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_DISCUSSION','A professor suggests that every first-year lecture should be recorded and posted online. Read the two viewpoints and write a response (about 100-115 words) giving your opinion and a supporting detail. Marcus: "I can pause and rewatch, so I finally understand the maths steps." — Hina: "If the lecture is online by Friday, nobody attends it on Wednesday."',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual review; a stated position, one developed reason, and a link to a viewpoint are expected.',109);
